        {
            "number": 2,
            "revision": "5dac916a0b413ffad2427a40d15e675639c33da2",
            "parents": [
                "ecb8a6351ef66a3925edcebafce73ddd94ca672a"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/13/90113/2",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Matt Spinler",
                "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                "username": "spinler"
            },
            "createdOn": 1778528339,
            "author": {
                "name": "Matt Spinler",
                "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                "username": "spinler"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "Shall we add an argument to indicate who is attempting to set the redundancy input? That would help identify who detected the Passive hardware issue, and we could use the redundancy setter logs for further analysis.\n\nThe parameter could be either a free-form string or an enum."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe we should be able to get the caller service name from the D-Bus message itself, but I am not sure whether the sdbusplus async infrastructure provides a way to access that information from the D-Bus message. @patrick@stwcx.xyz could share his view."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "While the sdbusplus message object does provide that info, that isn't exposed to the method call.\n\nUnless you feel strongly, I'm not too concerned about having it as a param since for us one call happens during the boot, and the other at runtime, so it will be easy to know where it came from if we did want to know.  Also, there would be errors logs for the root cause providing more info."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 5,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "> While the sdbusplus message object does provide that info, that isn't exposed to the method call.\n\nOK..\n\n> Unless you feel strongly, I'm not too concerned about having it as a param since for us one call happens during the boot, and the other at runtime, so it will be easy to know where it came from if we did want to know. Also, there would be errors logs for the root cause providing more info.\n\nIt would be nice to have but yes, we could look at the logs to decide who set/reset the redundancy input."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ramesh Iyyar",
                        "email": "rameshi1@in.ibm.com",
                        "username": "RameshIyyar"
                    },
                    "message": "Let\u2019s say the faulty BMC is replaced and redundancy is redetermined while the system is running. In that scenario, redundancy would be disabled, so when should it be enabled again?\n\nWould redundancy only be re-enabled after a system power off/on, or would the setter update the state to indicate that the Passive BMC is healthy again so redundancy can be enabled?\n\nAlso, how would the setter represent that state transition/reset? Currently, we have only defined the `PassiveHardwareProblem` enum."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Your questions made me realize I really have 2 distinct inputs here:\n\n1) Something is wrong with the connection between the passive BMC and its host processor.  This is the one that would be detected by the hypervisor at/near runtime.  This may be remedied by a passive BMC concurrent maintenance operation at runtime and so the input could be cleared as part of that.\n\n2) Something is wrong with the passive BMC's host processor itself so that it can't be operational, like\n - it is deconfigured\n - it doesn't have any attached memory\n - the chassis it's in can't be powered on so the host processor can't run\n\nThis one is really the case that can be cleared on a power off, because there is no chassis CM, and the BMC won't know if any of these are fixed, so it will take a boot to find out.  If not fixed, that same code that called setRedundancyInput before would just call it again to disable redundancy.  It was pointed out that it wouldn't be possible for that code to give an 'all clear' doing the boot, which is why we have to clear it before the boot.\n\nI will separate these out.\n\nI guess I may as well add a boolean parameter to set it to true or false as well to make it more future proof.\n\nI know there won't be any external caller to clear case 2) on a boot, so I will still handle it myself on the power off.\n\nI'm not sure yet about case 1) during the BMC CM, if someone will be able to call it to clear it or I would do it, but that can be dealt with when it comes up later."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/BMC/Redundancy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
